Mauritian food is the story of the island on a plate. French settlers and enslaved Africans and indentured labourers from India and Chinese migrants all brought their kitchens with them. Over two centuries each community borrowed from the others. The result is a cuisine that belongs to no single place. There are curries without coconut milk but with thyme and French stews cooked with chillies. Chinese noodles are sold from the same carts as Indian flatbreads.

## Street food

The cheapest way into Mauritian food is the street. The Economic Development Board puts street food prices from about USD 0.25 and names the favourites: Indian style flatbreads with spiced vegetables and Chinese dumplings and fried noodles.

- **Dalpuri**: a flatbread made with ground split peas and served with a curry of large peas and a tomato sauce.
- **Roti**: a plain flatbread with tomato sauce and pickles.
- **Fried noodles and fried rice** (*diri frir*) made popular by the island's Chinese community.
- **Boulettes**: fish balls and meatballs with chayote and other vegetables served in broth.

## Curries and rougaille and vindaye

- **Rougaille** is the island's signature tomato sauce. It is cooked with onions and garlic and chillies and ginger and eaten with fish or meat or vegetables.
- **Vindaye** (vinnday) is fish or octopus prepared with vinegar and mustard seeds and turmeric. It is a cousin of the Indian vindaloo.
- **Mauritian curries** usually leave out coconut milk often add European herbs such as thyme and are made with anything from duck to octopus.
- **Biryani** (*briani*) and **bol renversé** and **daube** (a French style stew) are everyday dishes too.
- Every table has its **chutneys and achards and pickles**.

## From the sea

Rice and seafood are the staples. Salted fish and **smoked blue marlin** and shrimp and octopus and prawns and freshwater crayfish (*camaron*) all turn up in Mauritian kitchens. Blue marlin is one of the fish the big game fishing boats of [Black River](/en/areas/black-river) go out for.

## Rum and tea

Sugar cane still grows across the west and rum is made from it. Mauritius has four distilleries (Grays and Medine and Chamarel and St Aubin) and two of them carry the names of places on this coast. Tea was planted at [Chamarel](/en/areas/chamarel) in the 19th century when the governor Sir John Pope Hennessy revived tea growing on the island.

## What it costs

The EDB's indicative prices: street food from about USD 0.25 and a meal for two in a moderately priced restaurant from USD 30. In a high end restaurant it starts from USD 50.

## Good to know

- Mauritian food can be hot: ask for *pa tro pima* (not too much chilli) if you prefer it mild.
- Chinese and Asian restaurants are found all around the island and a dinner out at one is a treat for many Mauritian families.
